WEBVTT

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:35.230
<v Ira Glass>OK, so you call a hotline, and then a complete stranger tries to figure out how to help you, on the spot. That idea seems to have begun in the 1950s. The first suicide hotline in the United States was created in the early '60s by a guy in San Francisco, who was a priest and also a journalist. And it was just him answering the phone at first.
<v Ira Glass>好的，当你拨打热线电话时，一个完全陌生的人会立刻想办法帮你。这个想法似乎起源于20世纪50年代。美国第一条自杀干预热线是20世纪60年代初由旧金山的一位牧师兼记者建立的。起初，只有他一个人在接电话。

00:00:35.230 --> 00:01:10.400
<v Ira Glass>Ads on matchbooks and sides of buses said, "Thinking of ending it all? Call Bruce," which, by the way, was not his real name. His real name was Bernard Mayes. But of course, the power of anonymity is so important to any hotline. People would call. And sometimes he could help them precisely because he had no connection to their life at all. Like, they could say anything to him. In those pre-internet days, that was completely new, to harness that kind of anonymity, the intimacy of it, this way, over the phone.
<v Ira Glass>火柴盒和公交车侧面的广告上写着：“想结束这一切吗？打给布鲁斯。”顺便说一下，那不是他的真名。他的真名是伯纳德·梅斯。但当然，匿名权对于任何热线来说都非常重要。人们会打电话。有时他能帮助他们，正是因为他与他们的生活毫无关联。他们可以对他畅所欲言。在互联网出现之前的那些日子里，通过电话利用这种匿名性和亲密感是全新的尝试。

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:32.690
<v Ira Glass>These days, of course, there are all kinds of hotlines for people in all sorts of situations-- prayer hotlines, psychic hotlines, also hotlines for homework help, for new moms. There's a hotline for owners of three-legged dogs, and another one specifically for anybody who swallows one of those-- you know those little, round button batteries? That one also handles any kid who pushes it up their nose.
<v Ira Glass>如今，当然，各类人群在各种情况下都有各式各样的热线——祈祷热线、通灵热线，还有家庭作业求助热线、新手妈妈热线。有一个专门为三条腿狗的主人准备的热线，还有一个专门为误吞了那些——你知道那些小圆纽扣电池的人准备的热线。那条热线也处理把电池塞进鼻孔的孩子的情况。

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:54.710
<v Ira Glass>Today we're going to devote our entire show to one phone call that happened on one hotline, a very unusual hotline. And then we have everything that followed from that one call. It takes you inside this world that I think either you're already in this world, or it's totally invisible to you. Like it's all around you. You don't even register that this world is there.
<v Ira Glass>今天，我们要把整期节目献给某条热线上的一个电话，那是一条非常不寻常的热线。接着，我们还会讲讲那个电话之后发生的一切。它将带你进入这个世界，我想你要么已经在其中，要么对它视而不见。它就像环绕在你身边。你甚至都没有意识到这个世界的存在。

00:01:54.710 --> 00:02:15.140
<v Ira Glass>Mary Harris tells what happened. She's the host of Slate's daily news podcast, What Next. One quick note, some parts of this phone call might not be great for young children to hear. I suppose I could have given you this warning before mentioning that part about pushing batteries up your nose. But anyway, here's Mary with Act 1, "The Call."
<v Ira Glass>玛丽·哈里斯讲述了发生的事情。她是Slate每日新闻播客《What Next》的主持人。简单提醒一下，这个通话中的某些部分可能不太适合年幼的孩子听。我想我本可以在提到把电池塞进鼻子里那部分之前就给你警告。但无论如何，这是玛丽带来的第一幕，“那通电话”。

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.270
<v Mary Harris>The call on this story took place a few years ago.
<v Mary Harris>这个故事里的通话发生在几年前。

